From c816026f86d4e7e2c54c97f56ec6ed6e9ed76903 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Revulate <76570121+Revulate@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 03:10:45 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat(gpui): platform-backed bidi text geometry and visual
 range spans

Add `ShapedLine::visual_spans_for_range`, which turns a logical UTF-8 byte
range into sorted, disjoint visual spans, plus checked caret APIs
(`x_for_index_with_affinity_checked`, `caret_for_x` with affinity) and
`bidi_geometry_complete`.

On Windows, `LineLayout` now carries interaction geometry separately from
glyph ink: `VisualCell`s and leading/trailing `Caret`s at every editable
DirectWrite cluster boundary. Boundaries come from DirectWrite's cluster
map intersected with Unicode extended-grapheme boundaries, so fallback
font splits never expose the inside of a combining sequence, ZWJ emoji or
variation selector, while ligatures stay grouped. Cells come from
`IDWriteTextLayout::HitTestTextRange` and carets from
`HitTestTextPosition`, so selection, hit testing and caret lookup no
longer infer rectangles from glyph origins. These per-cluster queries run
only for lines with an odd-bidi-level run; plain LTR text keeps the old
glyph path. The shaper honors `baselineOriginX`, `bidiLevel`, advances and
offsets when placing RTL glyphs, maps cluster-map entries to glyphs by
their first UTF-16 offset instead of `ClusterAnalyzer`, and uses
`widthIncludingTrailingWhitespace` as the line width.

Range and caret queries recover independently per cluster: two endpoint
carets synthesize a missing range, the direction-aware union of range
cells synthesizes a missing caret pair. Anything less degrades the line:
`bidi_geometry_complete` is false, one bounded warning is logged, and the
geometry APIs fail closed instead of returning logical-glyph positions.
A debug-only `GPUI_DIRECTWRITE_TEXT_GEOMETRY_FAULT` seam exercises these
paths; release builds construct only `GeometryFaultInjection::None`.

Every shaped glyph also carries a monotonic source-order advance
(`logical_x`, `logical_advance`) independent of its physical ink origin.
Line splits, including `ShapedLineCursor`, keep the physical bidi glyph
order and carry these fields, and wrapping is refused for lines with a
DirectWrite bidi run (reporting the full unwrapped width). macOS and wgpu
keep the glyph-based fallback.

DirectWrite draws tabs as zero-glyph runs whose cluster map points at a
glyph the run does not have, so those runs skip the cluster-map path.
As upstream does, each tab keeps an invisible space glyph; here it sits
at the tab's native hit-test box and carries the native tab width as its
logical advance, and each tab is its own editable cluster, so tabs keep
their advance and caret mapping in LTR and bidi lines alike. Upstream's
`tabs_preserve_native_advances_and_cursor` test checks LTR lines against
logical advances as before and checks bidi lines against DirectWrite's
own leading caret and tab cell width, since this geometry reports
physical carets.

`examples/platform_text_geometry.rs` (Windows, real platform text system,
now including tab lines) and `examples/visual_range_spans.rs`
(platform-independent fallback) exercise the contract. The obsolete
`ClusterAnalyzer` unit test is removed with the type it tested, and the
upstream `ShapedLine` tests in `text_system/line.rs` that build
`LineLayout` and `ShapedGlyph` literals fill the new geometry fields, so
gpui's lib tests compile again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
